Sources (all normative for semantics, later arbitrages complete earlier ones and are authoritative):
- spec MM-03.3 v0.2: ~/Downloads/ERN_Memory_Model_0.3.3.md
- pass-1 brief: ${SP}/task_mm03.md ; arbitrages A1-A11: ${SP}/task_mm03_p2.md ; B1-B10: ${SP}/task_mm03_p3.md ; C1-C9 and the pass-4 task: ${SP}/task_mm03_p4.md (French).
- pass-3 report (context, decisions already reported): ${REPO}/formal/reports/MM03_v0.1_p3.md
- statement diff pass 3 -> pass 4 (theorems removed/changed, cut at top-level :=): ${SP}/p4/stmtdiff.txt ; pass-3 module snapshot: ${SP}/p4/p3.lean
- see the code diff with: git -C ${REPO} diff 31d90e3..HEAD -- formal/Ern/MM03.lean
Rules of the project are in ${REPO}/AGENTS.md (section Review). Key ones: a definition says what its rule says, not stronger nor weaker; a theorem that holds because a definition is too permissive or a hypothesis unsatisfiable is blocking; no statement weakened; every formalized rule cites its location; no interpretation chosen silently where the source is unclear; nothing outside the task.
Constraints for you: do NOT edit any file in the repo, do NOT run 'lake build' (the module is already built). To test a claim, write a scratch file in ${SP}/rev8/<your-lens>/ starting with 'import Ern.MM03' and 'namespace Ern.MM03', and run: cd ${REPO}/formal && ~/.elan/bin/lake env lean <file>. Lean core only (no Mathlib). Every finding needs evidence: a quote from the sources with its location, a Lean counterexample you checked, or command output. No evidence, no finding. Do not ask for additions beyond the task. Report only real defects; an empty list is a valid answer.`

const FINDINGS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'major', 'minor'] },
          location: { type: 'string' },
          cause: { type: 'string', enum: ['formalization error', 'missing coverage', 'spec defect', 'design decision to report'] },
          evidence: { type: 'string' },
          suggested_fix: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['title', 'severity', 'location', 'cause', 'evidence'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['findings'],
}

const VERDICTS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    verdicts: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['confirmed', 'refuted', 'partly'] },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'major', 'minor'] },
          reasoning: { type: 'string' },
          evidence: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['title', 'verdict', 'reasoning'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['verdicts'],
}

const LENSES = [
  { key: 'c1', prompt: `Lens C1 (effective anchors). Check S3.effAnchor, EffAnchorOK, AnchorOrder, DependPre, the B8 clause of TransferPre, and the AnchorOrder preservation section (effAnchor_* lemmas, AnchorOrder.of, *_preserves_anchorOrder, exit_preserves_anchorOrder), and reachable_access_invariant_underDD concluding WF3 and full Inv3. Questions: does AnchorOrder say exactly C1 (for every usable q and EVERY anchor x of q, the frame of the effective anchor of x is the frame of q or an ancestor)? Is EffAnchorOK too strong or too weak (it requires A[effAnchor x] to exist and its holder to have a frame; it does not require activity)? The implementation also checks the B8 clause of transfer for dependents that move (q = dst or q owned within src), not only when the effective anchor moves: is that a reading C1/B8 do not allow, and does it reject programs the spec accepts? Cases 10 and n9d are now rejected at transfer instead of exit. ao2 must be rejected at depend and case 8 stay ok (theorem ao2_rejected_at_depend, case 8 examples).` },
  { key: 'c2', prompt: `Lens C2 (NoDangling). Check NoDangling, Inv3 = Inv3base and AnchorOrder and NoDangling, noDangling_iff, NoDangling.of, every *_preserves_noDangling (derive, split, release, depend, write3, pinEff, transfer, enter, alloc, exit, endUse, free, issue, reset), usable_of_part, WF3.deadOf, exit_owned_of_usable, endUse_active, free_active, reachable_no_dangling. C2 requires: preservation by every operation; exitFrame_no_dangling and free_no_dangling become lemmas of that preservation without weakening their statements (compare with ${SP}/p4/p3.lean). Is NoDangling exactly C2 (no active authority covers a leaf of a place that is not usable in the sense of Usable; nothing on initialisation)? Is any operation missing? Are the witness theorems that state Inv3 still correct, in particular transfer_not_inv3_preserving whose statement was changed to (Inv3base and AnchorOrder) and not NoDangling: is that change justified or a weakening?` },
  { key: 'c3c4c5c8', prompt: `Lens C3, C4, C5, C8. Check: WF3 without RootsDisjoint (C3; rootsDisjoint_always stays a theorem; RootHeld stays in WF3); ReleasePre and HolderRight (C4: reject an authority without origin; a derived authority is releasable if held by the current frame, or by a place p on which the current frame has exercisable Modify; HasRight is the B2 notion of exercisable right); EndUsePre (C5: reject every pinned ending leaf, dynamic or static, ExternalDone only in unpin); StaticPinnedBottom and static_pinned_never_exits (C5: every leaf covered by K.Pinned belongs to a place owned transitively by the base frame; hypothesis only of the theorems that need it; implemented with an existential over places and base frame = s.F.getLastD 0); C8 (endUse p leaves p usable with Uninit leaves; only places owned by p die). Check whether the ExternalDone uses elsewhere (unpin, free, exit) are consistent with C5. Check the docstrings cite C3/C4/C5/C8.` },
  { key: 'c6c9', prompt: `Lens C6, C9 (alloc with a shape, Satisfiable, issue alignment). Check shapeOwner, shapeP, ShapeOK, enterP_eq_shapeP, AllocStatic, allocP, allocEff, AllocStep, K.Satisfiable type, all alloc_* theorems (alloc_preserves, alloc_rootChain, alloc_rootHeld, alloc_fresh, alloc_owned_root, alloc_owned_root_leaf, alloc_usable_new, alloc_preserves_anchorOrder, alloc_preserves_noDangling), the arena transport case for alloc, and MetaRight. C6: alloc provider owner shape with outcomes ok/fail/reject, shape in the same encoding as the locals of enterFrame (indexed forest, parents of lower index), abstract (no alignment or layout); requires Init leaves on the provider; the effect sets the provider metadata to Init; issue aligned: MetaRight also accepts exercisable Modify on the arena value. C9: K.Satisfiable provider shape s depends on the current state; no theorem assumes it true. Questions: the implementation gives the owning-element component of a shape element the enterFrame meaning (an element owned by an earlier element), roots owned by owner: is that a silent interpretation? The provider metadata is read as the leaves of the provider: justified by B5's ProviderRight? With the Init precondition the metadata write is a no-op on I: is that faithful? Were the pass-3 alloc theorems rewritten without weakening (alloc_fresh generalised; alloc_owned_root split into a general theorem without the footprint-self conjunct plus alloc_owned_root_leaf with exactly the pass-3 conjuncts for the one-leaf shape)?` },
  { key: 'integrity', prompt: `Lens statement integrity and cases. Use ${SP}/p4/stmtdiff.txt (11 removed, 20 changed theorem statements) and the code diff. For every removed or changed statement decide whether it is weakened (hypothesis added, conclusion dropped, domain narrowed) without a justification that the brief forces; note that theorems whose text did not change can still change meaning because definitions changed (Inv3, WF3, ReleasePre, EndUsePre, AllocStep, MetaRight, TransferPre, DependPre): look for any such theorem that became vacuous or weaker. Check that counterexamples of passes 1-3 are kept (the brief: contre-exemples conserves) or replaced with a justification. Check the pass-4 cases required by the brief (section 5 of task_mm03_p4.md) are all present in the module with the expected verdicts (section 'New cases of pass 4' and the ao2/case 8/rq1/mv0/mv1 examples) and that every case of passes 1-3 is still there; list verdict changes of old cases (cases 10 and n9d now rejected at transfer, rq1 release, KE endUse, mv0/mv1 alloc). Also check: no sorry/admit/axiom/native_decide/noncomputable, docstrings cite the clause or arbitrage for every new definition and theorem encoding a rule.` },
]

phase('Find')
const results = await pipeline(
  LENSES,
  l => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYour lens: ${l.prompt}\n\nReturn your findings.`, { label: `find:${l.key}`, phase: 'Find', schema: FINDINGS }),
  (found, l) => {
    const fs = (found && found.findings) || []
    if (fs.length === 0) return { lens: l.key, findings: [], verdicts: [] }
    return agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are the judge for lens ${l.key}. Another reviewer reported the findings below. Try hard to REFUTE each one: re-read the sources and the code yourself, check every quoted passage and every Lean claim (run counterexamples). A finding survives only with real evidence. Default to refuted when the evidence does not hold, when the brief explicitly allows the behaviour, or when the point was already settled by an arbitrage. Re-assess severity for survivors.\n\nFindings:\n${JSON.stringify(fs, null, 2)}`,
      { label: `judge:${l.key}`, phase: 'Judge', schema: VERDICTS })
      .then(v => ({ lens: l.key, findings: fs, verdicts: (v && v.verdicts) || [] }))
  }
)
return results.filter(Boolean)

